course: dictionary
lesson: 11015
title: Waterproof: one word, three jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Waterproof can be an adjective, a noun or a verb, and the sentence tells you which. «Penguins keep warm with tightly packed waterproof feathers.» Here waterproof describes the feathers, so it is an adjective meaning that water cannot get in or through.
explain: «I zipped up my waterproof before the hike.» Here a waterproof is a noun: a coat that keeps the rain off. In the UK, waterproofs can also mean a whole set of rain clothes.
explain: «You can waterproof a jacket with a special spray.» Here waterproof is a verb, an action word: it means to treat something so water cannot get through. Try each meaning in the sentence to see which one fits.
fact: Waterproof means not letting water in or through.
fact: A waterproof is a coat that keeps the rain off (UK).
fact: To waterproof something means to treat it so water cannot get through.
fact: In science, plastic and rubber are waterproof, while paper and cotton absorb water.
fact: Animals can be waterproof too: penguins have tightly packed waterproof feathers, but «donkey coats are not waterproof» like horses' coats.
exercise: «Penguins keep warm with tightly packed waterproof feathers.» What does waterproof mean here? | Able to swim very fast | Shiny and bright | Warm and fluffy | *Water cannot get in or through
exercise: «Pack your waterproof, the forecast says rain.» What is a waterproof here? | A type of walking boot | A glass coating on a mug | *A coat that keeps the rain off | A bag full of water
exercise: In «You can waterproof a jacket with a special spray», what kind of word is waterproof? | *A verb, an action word | An adverb, telling how | An adjective, describing a thing | A noun, a name for a coat
test: «Donkey coats are not waterproof like horses' coats.» What does this tell you? | *Rain can get through donkey coats, so donkeys need shelter | Donkeys have the same coats as horses | Donkey coats are always dry | Donkeys do not like rain at all
test: Which sentence uses waterproof as a noun? | «The mug was waterproof after its glaze.» | «The waterproof feathers kept the penguin warm.» | *«I zipped up my waterproof before we set off.» | «Waterproof boots squeak on wet floors.»
exam: Jayden says, «The map is waterproof.» What kind of word is waterproof here? | An adverb, telling how | *An adjective, describing the map | A noun, a thing | A verb, an action
